@dev Component 10.6: a holder reporting a compromised key cannot wait
     for a quorum. Suspension is reversible, so one signature is enough;
     the revocation that may follow still needs the quorum. The reason
     is committed to as for a proposal. A revoked TI may still do
     this, on purpose: withdrawing its certificates starts here,
     and a verifier reading only this record would otherwise see VALID.

@title Ballots
@notice What a status proposal and a rule change have in common: who
        opened it, when, who signed it, and where it stands. Signing,
        quorum, the timelock, the veto and expiry work the same way for both.

@dev A change is valid if applying it now leaves every policy reachable,
      and every timelock within the proposal lifetime. Asked again at
      execution, so two changes approved apart cannot break it together.

@title RuleChanges
@notice Self-administration (component 10.4): the contract changes its own
        signers, policies and proposal lifetime only through a quorum of its
        own, under the ADMIN policy. No single administrator can add a
        signer, lower a threshold or shorten a timelock alone.
@dev Each change is its own function, so a signer's wallet shows what it is
     being asked to approve by name.

@dev Sets the certificate's standing: SUSPENDED, REVOKED (for
      good), or VALID again. This is what a verifier reads; the
      back-office catches up via `ProposalExecuted`.

@dev Permissionless: expiry is a mechanical consequence of the clock,
      not a decision, and leaving it to signers would let a certificate
      stay blocked indefinitely.

@title StatusProposals
@notice Proposals to suspend, revoke or reinstate a certificate (component
        10.5), and their audit verification (component 10.7). An executed
        proposal sets the certificate's standing in the registry.

@dev Runs once, when the implementation is deployed: what it wrote to
